<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Assia Djebar</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1936">1936</a>–<a href="/years/2015">2015</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as Fatima-Zohra Imalayène</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Assia Djebar (1936–2015) was an Algerian novelist, historian and film-maker, the pen name of Fatima-Zohra Imalayène. Writing in French, she explored the lives of Algerian women and the memory of colonial conquest and war in novels such as Fantasia: An Algerian Cavalcade. In 2005 she became the first writer from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maghreb">Maghreb</a> elected to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/academie-francaise">Académie française</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/assia-djebar.jpg" alt="Assia Djebar" width="320" height="308" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Michel-georges bernard · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Assia_Djebar.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Fatima-Zohra Imalayen was born on 30 June 1936 in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cherchell">Cherchell</a>, a small seaport near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/algiers">Algiers</a>, to Tahar Imalhayène and Bahia Sahraoui, a family of Chenoua Berber origin. Her father taught French at the primary school in Mouzaïaville that she attended. She later went to a Quranic private boarding school in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/blida">Blida</a>, where she was one of only two girls, and studied at the Collège de Blida, where she was the only Muslim in her class. In 1955 she entered the École normale supérieure de jeunes filles, the first Algerian and Muslim woman educated at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>'s most elite schools. Her studies were interrupted by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/algerian-war">Algerian War</a>, and she continued them in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tunis">Tunis</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>In 1957 she chose the pen name Assia Djebar for her first novel, La Soif. Les Impatients followed in 1958. She taught at the University of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rabat">Rabat</a> from 1959 to 1962 and then at the University of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/algiers">Algiers</a>, where she became head of the French section. She returned to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/algeria">Algeria</a> in 1962 and published Les Enfants du Nouveau Monde; Les Alouettes naïves came in 1967. She lived in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a> from 1965 to 1974 and married the Algerian poet Malek Alloula in 1980. From 1997 to 2001 she directed the Center of French and Francophone Studies at Louisiana State University. She also made films, among them La Nouba des femmes du Mont Chenoua in 1977 and La Zerda ou les chants de l'oubli in 1979.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Works and ideas</h2><p>Most of Djebar's works deal with obstacles faced by women, and she is noted for a feminist stance. One critic describes her novels as focused on creating a genealogy of Algerian women, with a politics both anti-patriarchal and anti-colonial. In L'Amour, la fantasia of 1985, translated as Fantasia: An Algerian Cavalcade, she repeatedly states her ambivalence about language and about her identity as a Western-educated, Algerian, feminist, Muslim intellectual. She won the Neustadt International Prize for Literature in 1996 and the Peace Prize of the German Book Trade in 2000.</p><p>On 16 June 2005 she was elected to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/academie-francaise">Académie française</a>, the first writer from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maghreb">Maghreb</a> to be so honoured and the fifth woman to join. She died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a> in February 2015, aged 78.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Assia Djebar” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Writers born in the colony, among them Albert Camus, Kateb Yacine, Mohammed Dib and <b>Assia Djebar</b>, and the large Algerian, pied-noir and harki communities in France keep its memory alive and contested.</blockquote><a href="/empire/french-algeria">French Algeria · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cherchell">Cherchell</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/algeria">Algeria</a>. Died in 19th arrondissement of Paris, in what is now <a href="/countries/france">France</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=36.61,2.19&amp;year=1987" aria-label="Cherchell on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Assia Djebar"><image href="/maps/globes/g000n30.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5150 4435h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5150 4435h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=36.61,2.19&amp;year=1987">What was here in 1987? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
